1043 #edb/ref 17592186125328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124702 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124702 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124702 #edb/ref 1045 "Level Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124702 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124702 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124702 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "97435fa2-2414-5733-b9c9-109a6d98298e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125014 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=-1$ gives a line\n\nII. $c=0$ gives a parabola and the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$\n\nIII. $c=1$ gives two lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "592215bc-9340-546e-9e8f-8c90c87cd2d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125014 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x & =c \\\\ xy({x}^{2}-y) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=\\pm 1,$ which are not lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the parabola $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nSo, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125326 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125326 #edb/ref 1061 15418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125326 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "76440382-82ba-5d62-8fa4-df79b4f447e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125326 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125327 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125327 #edb/ref 1061 15419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125327 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5630589a-ec0b-5b7d-9643-ba6b2dadf69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125327 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125328 #edb/ref 1061 15420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125328 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41eaaf34-192a-5179-a455-5ee546c5e672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125328 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125456 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125456 #edb/ref 1061 22187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125456 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c173f9a1-f861-58bd-a38c-880ac35a93c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125456 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125753 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ with the plane $z=4.$\n\nTo find the intersection, we set both equations equal to each other:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4\n$$\n\nGraphing the above equation in the $xy$-plane, we get a circle $\\color{black} C$ with center at $(0,0)$ and radius $2.$\n\n![](images/c3/c32f35ccd2c0eb01732194ac419fa328f62f1281c8bf4fb5ccc75218153c46d5.png)\n\nThe curve  $\\color{black} C$ is called a **level curve** of the surface $z = x^2 + y^2.$ The level curves of the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = c\n$$\n\nfor $c=1,2,3,4$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c86604edc24e92fe51a8eed55386d8b01531b4a4f025e70f7ff33caf0c5df81.png)\n\nThe diagram above is called a **contour plot.** The level curves are sometimes called **contours.**\n\nIn general, a **level curve** $L_{c}(f)$ of a real-valued function of two variables $f(x,y)$ is any set of the form\n\n \n\n$$\nL_{c}(f) = \\left\\{(x,y) \\, : \\,f(x,y)=c\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a constant.\n\n**Note:** In our example, $x^2 +y^2 \\ge 0$ for all $(x,y),$ which means that the sets $L_{c}(f)$ are nonempty for $c \\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125753 #edb/ref 1065 2876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125753 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3a86e054-2c30-5b05-8ec7-9293b3a78aa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125753 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260656 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ef/ef27f12eb1ee6872210bf8cadb6908efd31c1bb54e9e7d34cc3456df2ee03a77.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260656 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260656 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b8f524a-9681-5a5a-aa6a-8d21d370c6a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261134 #edb/ref 1080 "images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261134 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261134 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90a9daad-d37b-5bdf-8f0c-1e578a2870e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261280 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2c/2c570cc9958db69a30dc400920b6ff27574e79bd26a53b35e086cb03034c5b6d.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a19c1461-ef0c-529e-9726-029d4c772ce7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261596 #edb/ref 1080 "images/cd/cdd8cec2d30ff2956d5210a49036c17d99781d0b62c0b0f42f97d0575c4d80f6.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c30db028-ad8b-5802-9d7d-fe2ee0d636d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261746 #edb/ref 1080 "images/d7/d774c444ad33c68ec5d507611324030f2005671265ba5f855f322d8db57b69b4.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261746 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261746 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d4894fee-b5e1-59e2-86ca-ba516e7266bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186261596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "93f1dce4-27ec-501b-8cb3-8ca95b44d739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262943 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262943 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae6e3096-5c3b-5202-84b9-abfe5e790c65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262943 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-3,3].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-3,3].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =c \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\frac{c}{3}+2\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that the equations $x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\frac{c}{3}+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-3,3]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cdd8cec2d30ff2956d5210a49036c17d99781d0b62c0b0f42f97d0575c4d80f6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336131 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336131 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336131 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a905dcc-7062-5ba9-9481-732d5b5264b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337363 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337363 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337363 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92663878-d31f-5b9a-82dc-e0531f783d7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337462 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337462 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337462 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e18aeef-f61b-5c4d-a0f7-d57397008dc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337538 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337538 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337538 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5ece99e-e9d1-5800-b5d5-2a569fbee7c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337739 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337739 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337739 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c045c018-7da4-5278-a1f9-dc81aff7cf6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186337363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338631 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4f5ebcf-28da-59c5-ae3f-90358c258495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338937 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338937 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71395" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338937 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338937 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a line\n\nII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola\n\nIII. $c=0$ gives two lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b94a6f8a-60a9-5806-9e18-4814d39c5835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338937 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ which is not a line.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=4,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}},$ which consists of two lines.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387992 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/03/037422e7b1ce3b3d9067aba39c4fa85c6311770e63e70758c34aa61629c7f58e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ec437fa-7d05-53d2-8259-19727f28f519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387992 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =c \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡c+2\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that the equations $y=-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186387996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387993 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5053c438-eb23-5e9c-bd54-db15189721dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387994 #edb/ref 1080 "images/56/56e923e4c1e2603c9bd3c621df0a5a4043fcc6a37ac73427be14541407fc19d8.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387994 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387994 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69ffca37-cace-5fde-98f2-279fdf4f3975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387995 #edb/ref 1080 "images/09/09f1a18e1a6fdcd29a40026cbffd8e8bdec23511bb692a1e920c18f942042470.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387995 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387995 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ef1d420-a12a-56ca-a25f-8624b9640b47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387996 #edb/ref 1080 "images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387996 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387996 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6719fa96-fc32-59ff-ad37-8586f87f620f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387997 #edb/ref 1080 "images/aa/aa4d0cd189808c717648640c5543a632c099970a